WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.950
<v Lecturer>Now let's talk about CloudFront.</v>

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.950
CloudFront is a content delivery network or CDN.

3
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.580
So anytime you see CDN in the exam, think CloudFront.

4
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.940
It improves the read performance by caching the content

5
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.030
of your website at the different edge locations.

6
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.060
And because your content is cached all around the world,

7
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.620
then your users all around the world

8
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:20.980
will have a lower latency

9
00:00:21.977 --> 00:00:23.700
and this will improve the user experience.

10
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.560
CloudFront is made of hundreds of

11
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.990
points of presence globally and that includes

12
00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.320
edge locations and caches across the world.

13
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.770
On top of it, by having the content distributed globally,

14
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.870
we are getting DDoS protection.

15
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.720
So DDoS is a sort of attack where all your servers

16
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.580
around the world are getting attacked at the same time.

17
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.470
We'll see this later on in this course,

18
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.580
and the idea is that CloudFront,

19
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.710
because your application is worldwide,

20
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.630
then you're protected against these attacks

21
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.490
also using something called Shield

22
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.140
and Web Application Firewall

23
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.570
that we will be seeing in the security section.

24
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.270
So if you wanna look at a map of the world,

25
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.330
these are the map and we see some edge locations

26
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.800
as well as edge caches.

27
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.870
And so say we had created an S3 bucket

28
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.927
and a website on our S3 bucket in Australia,

29
00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:09.660
but we had a user maybe in America.

30
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.040
Then what the user will do is that

31
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.570
it will request the content

32
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.450
from an American edge location using CloudFront

33
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.890
and CloudFront will be able to fetch

34
00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.720
the content from Australia.

35
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.400
Now, if another user in the US

36
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.230
will be requesting the same content,

37
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.270
then it will be served directly from the edge

38
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.920
and it will not go all the way to Australia

39
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.270
to serve that content.

40
00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.970
Same if a user is in China, then it'll be talking

41
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.130
to a Chinese point of presence

42
00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.620
and then redirected to the S3 buckets,

43
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.770
and then the content will be cached at the edge.

44
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.830
CloudFront has several origins, which are basically backends

45
00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.900
that you want CloudFront to connect to.

46
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.330
For example, we have an Amazon S3 bucket.

47
00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.340
So this is used for distributing files

48
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.410
and caching them at the edge.

49
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.590
Or to upload files directly into Amazon S3

50
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.600
through Amazon CloudFront.

51
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.350
And the access between CloudFront and Amazon S3

52
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.880
is secured using something called

53
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.760
origin access control or OAC.

54
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.330
You can also connect CloudFront to anything within your VPC.

55
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.520
So it's called a VPC origin,

56
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.350
and it's for applications that are hosted

57
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.890
within your network on Amazon, within the private subnets.

58
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.440
So it could be a private application balancer,

59
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.750
it could be a private network load balancer,

60
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.750
or even private EC2 instances.

61
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.270
You can also connect to a custom origin

62
00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.040
over the HTTP protocol,

63
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.320
and it could be, for example, on the S3 website,

64
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.250
you first must enable the bucket as a static S3 website,

65
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.220
or it could be any public HTTP backend you want.

66
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.770
For example, if you have a public load balancer,

67
00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.860
you can connect it directly into CloudFront.

68
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.870
So at a high level, how does CloudFront work?

69
00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.960
We have the edge location all around the world, okay?

70
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.210
And then it will be connecting to your origin.

71
00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.990
So would it be an S3 bucket or an HTTP server.

72
00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.610
And when the client connects

73
00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.240
and does an HTTP request into your edge location,

74
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.980
then the edge location will see if it has it in the cache.

75
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.630
If it doesn't have it in the cache,

76
00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.530
then it will go to the origin to get the request results.

77
00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.300
And then once it retrieves the results,

78
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.400
it will be caching it into a local cache

79
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.980
so that if another client requests the same content

80
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.410
from the same edge location, then the edge location

81
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.260
does not need to go to the origin.

82
00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.590
So if we have S3 as an origin, then if we look at the cloud,

83
00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.290
your S3 bucket is your origin in some region,

84
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.090
and then you have edge locations all around the world,

85
00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.460
for example, at Los Angeles,

86
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.610
and your users accessing the edge location in Los Angeles

87
00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.100
will get their content directly served

88
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.390
through the edge location.

89
00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.190
But first, the edge location will get it

90
00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.090
from the origin S3 bucket through the private network.

91
00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.830
And the S3 bucket will be secured

92
00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.750
using an origin access control

93
00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.680
and by modifying the S3 bucket policy on the S3 bucket.

94
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.980
So this is the same when we have a user in Sao Paulo,

95
00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.240
for example, in Brazil.

96
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.910
Again, this will be another edge location,

97
00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.610
which will be serving users close to Brazil,

98
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.590
and then it will be a private connection

99
00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.520
between your edge location and your S3 bucket and so on.

100
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.800
So using CloudFront and the edge locations,

101
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.400
we can see that the content of our S3 bucket in one region

102
00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.860
can be distributed all around the world

103
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.070
through the edge locations or points of presence.

104
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.290
So one question that's common is what is the difference

105
00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.560
between CloudFront and something like S3 replication?

106
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.790
Well, if you have CloudFront,

107
00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.560
you're using the Global Edge network.

108
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.740
So this is about 216 points of presence.

109
00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.870
And the files are going to be cached

110
00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.420
in each edge location maybe for a day.

111
00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.640
So this is amazing if you have static content

112
00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.830
that must be able to be available

113
00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.870
everywhere around the world.

114
00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.090
S3 cross region replication is different.

115
00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.710
It must be set up for each region

116
00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.030
you want replication to happen.

117
00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.370
So this is not for every region in the world.

118
00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.830
And then files are going to be updated in near real time

119
00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.540
so there's no caching that happens.

120
00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.250
And it is only for read only.

121
00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.770
And so this is great if you have dynamic content

122
00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.000
that needs to change all the time

123
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.240
and be available at low latency in a few regions.

124
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.950
So these are very different purposes.

125
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:05.670
CloudFront is a CDN, which is to cache content

126
00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.880
all around the world, whereas S3 cross region replication

127
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.510
is to really replicate an entire bucket into another region.

128
00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.060
So hopefully that makes sense about CloudFront.

129
00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:15.810
In the next lecture we'll have a play

130
00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.180
and see how we can set up a CloudFront distribution

131
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.070
on the CloudFront and S3 buckets.

132
00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.920
So we'll see you in the next lecture.

